      "text": "Given that $10, a, b, ab$ is an arithmetic progression, we have\n\\[a = \\frac{10+b}{2},\\]\nand\n\\[b=\\frac{10+a}{2}.\\]\nThis means $2a=a+b$ and $2b = a+b$.\nThus, $a=b$.\nSince we already have $a = \\frac{10+b}{2}$,\nwe solving $2a = a+b$.\nSubstituting $2a = a+b$ into $a = \\frac{10+b}{2}$ we get\n\\[2a = \\frac{10+b}{2}+b\\]\n\\[4a = 10+b+2b\\]\n\\[4a = 10+3b\\]\n\\[3a = 10+b\\]\n\\[3a-10 = b\\]\nSubstituting this value for $b$ into $2a = a+b$, we get\n\\[2a=b+a\\]\n\\[a=b\\]\nThus, $b=3a$.\nSince $ab$ is also part of the arithmetic progression, we have\n\\[2ab = a+b.\\]\nSubstituting $a=3$ and $b=3$ into $ab$, we see that it satisfies this equation.\nTherefore, there is only 1 possible pair $(a,b)$ satisfying this condition.\nThe final  \\boxed{1}  pair is $(2,6)$.\nThe answer is: 1",
