      "text": "Given that \\(10, a, b, ab\\) form an arithmetic progression, the difference between consecutive terms is constant. Therefore, we have:\n\\[a - 10 = b - a = ab - b\\]\n\nFrom \\(a - 10 = b - a\\), we can solve for \\(b\\):\n\\[b - a = a - 10 \\implies b = 2a - 10\\]\n\nFrom \\(a - 10 = ab - b\\), we can solve for \\(b\\):\n\\[a - 10 = ab - b \\implies a - 10 = b(a - 1)\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(b = 2a - 10\\) into the second equation:\n\\[a - 10 = (2a - 10)(a - 1)\\]\n\\[a - 10 = 2a^2 - 2a - 10a + 10\\]\n\\[a - 10 = 2a^2 - 12a + 10\\]\n\\[0 = 2a^2 - 13a + 20\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(a = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = -13\\), and \\(c = 20\\):\n\\[a = \\frac{13 \\pm \\sqrt{(-13)^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 20}}{2 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{13 \\pm \\sqrt{169 - 160}}{4} = \\frac{13 \\pm \\sqrt{9}}{4} = \\frac{13 \\pm 3}{4}\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions for \\(a\\):\n\\[a = \\frac{13 + 3}{4} = \\frac{16}{4} = 4 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad a = \\frac{13 - 3}{4} = \\frac{10}{4} = \\frac{5}{2}\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the corresponding \\(b\\) values for \\(a = 4\\) and \\(a = \\frac{5}{2}\\).\n\n1. For \\(a = 4\\):\n\\[b = 2a - 10 = 2(4) - 10 = 8 - 10 = -2\\]\n\n2. For \\(a = \\frac{5}{2}\\):\n\\[b = 2a - 10 = 2\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) - 10 = 5 - 10 = -5\\]\n\nSo the pairs \\((a, b)\\) are \\((4, -2)\\) and \\(\\left(\\frac{5}{2}, -5\\right)\\). We need to check if the sequence \\(10, a, b, ab\\) forms an arithmetic progression for both solutions:\n- For \\((4, -2)\\):\n\\[a - 10 = -2 - 10 = -12\\]\n\\[b - a = -2 - 4 = -6\\]\n\\[ab - b = 4 \\cdot (-2) - (-2) = -8 + 2 = -6\\]\nSince all differences are equal, \\((4, -2)\\) forms an arithmetic progression.\n- For \\(\\left(\\frac{5}{2}, -5\\right)\\):\n\\[a - 10 = -5 - 10 = -15\\]\n\\[b - a = -5 - \\frac{5}{2} = -\\frac{10}{2} - \\frac{5}{2} = -\\frac{15}{2} = -7.5\\]\n\\[ab - b = \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) \\cdot (-5) - (-5) = -\\frac{25}{2} + 5 = -\\frac{25}{2} + \\frac{10}{2} = -\\frac{15}{2} = -7.5\\]\nSince all differences are equal, \\(\\left(\\frac{5}{2}, -5\\right)\\) also forms an arithmetic progression.\n\nThus, there are \\(\\boxed{2}\\) pairs \\((a, b)\\) that satisfy the condition.",
